@page "/components/colorpicker"
@page "/components/color-picker"

<PageOutlet Url="components/colorpicker"
            Title="ColorPicker"
            Description="A keyboard-accessible color picker with a saturation-brightness area, hue and alpha sliders, text fields in hex, RGB, HSL or HSV, preset swatches, the browser's screen eyedropper, a translatable set of accessible names, and a value that reads and writes hex, rgb, hsl, hsv, hwb and oklch." />

<DemoPage Name="ColorPicker"
          Description="BitColorPicker is the panel a color is chosen on: drag the saturation-brightness area for the shade, the hue slider for the color it is a shade of, and the alpha slider for how much of it shows through. The exact value can be typed into the text fields instead - in hexadecimal, RGB, HSL or HSV, with an optional button that moves between them - picked in one click from a palette of presets, or sampled from anywhere on the screen with the browser's own eyedropper. The value is read and written in whatever notation it arrives in - hexadecimal in three, four, six or eight digits, rgb(), hsl(), hwb(), the perceptual oklab() and oklch() modern design tokens are written in, a color keyword like 'tomato', plus hsv() for the model the picker itself is built on - or pinned to one with Format, with a matching alpha-carrying notation for when a single string has to survive a transparent color. Everything on it is reachable from the keyboard, including the two-dimensional area, every part of it announces the color by name rather than by channel numbers, every string it writes for itself is translatable through Texts, and the whole panel mirrors in right-to-left."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubUrl="Inputs/ColorPicker/BitColorPicker.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="Inputs/ColorPicker/BitColorPickerDemo.razor">
    <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" Id="example1">
        <div>
            On its own the picker keeps its own state, starting on opaque white. <b>Label</b> names it - through
            <b>aria-labelledby</b> rather than a <b>label</b> element, since a panel of many controls has no single
            input a <b>for</b> could point at - and <b>LabelTemplate</b> takes markup where a string will not do.
            <b>IsEnabled="false"</b> greys the panel out and takes it out of the interaction; <b>ReadOnly</b> keeps
            it at full contrast and in the tab order, so the color can still be read out, and only refuses to
            change it.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <div>Basic:</div>
            <BitColorPicker />
            <br />
            <div>Label:</div>
            <BitColorPicker Label="Brand color" />
            <br />
            <div>LabelTemplate:</div>
            <BitColorPicker>
                <LabelTemplate>
                    <BitIcon IconName="@BitIconName.Color" /> <b>Accent color</b>
                </LabelTemplate>
            </BitColorPicker>
            <br />
            <div>Disabled:</div>
            <BitColorPicker IsEnabled="false" Color="#B34D4D" />
            <br />
            <div>ReadOnly:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ReadOnly ShowInputs Color="#4D7FB3" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Alpha & Preview" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
        <div>
            <b>ShowAlphaSlider</b> adds the transparency track under the hue slider and <b>ShowPreview</b> the swatch
            beside them. Both are drawn over a checkerboard, so a half-transparent color reads as half-transparent
            rather than as a paler solid. The alpha is tracked either way and always readable through <b>Alpha</b>;
            the slider only decides whether the user gets to change it.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <div>Alpha slider and preview:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowAlphaSlider ShowPreview @bind-Color="alphaColor" @bind-Alpha="alphaValue" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @alphaColor &nbsp; Alpha: @alphaValue</BitLabel>
            <br />
            <div>Preview only:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowPreview @bind-Color="previewColor" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @previewColor</BitLabel>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Inputs" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
        <div>
            <b>ShowInputs</b> adds the text fields, which is how an exact color is entered - or read off - without
            hunting for it on the gradient. Each commits when it is left or when Enter is pressed, and a value that
            is not a color is refused and replaced by the current one. The hexadecimal field grows to eight digits
            once the alpha slider is on, and gains an <b>A%</b> field beside the channels.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <b>InputsMode</b> decides which channels they are written in - hexadecimal with RGB, either alone, or the
            HSL and HSV triplets - and it is about how the color is typed, not how it is published: a picker edited
            in HSL still answers in whatever <b>Format</b> says. <b>ShowInputsModeSwitch</b> hands that choice to the
            user with a button that moves the fields on to the next set, and <b>@@bind-InputsMode</b> follows them
            there. Because the switch only changes which numbers the color is read as, it keeps working on a
            read-only picker.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <div>Hex and RGB, with alpha (an eight-digit hex and an A% field):</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowInputs ShowPreview ShowAlphaSlider Format="BitColorFormat.HexAlpha" @bind-Color="inputsAlphaColor" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @inputsAlphaColor</BitLabel>
            <br />
            <div>The user picks the model (try the button at the end of the row):</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowInputs ShowPreview ShowInputsModeSwitch
                            @bind-InputsMode="inputsMode" @bind-Color="inputsModeColor" />
            <BitLabel>Mode: @inputsMode &nbsp; Color: @inputsModeColor</BitLabel>
            <br />
            <div>Fixed to HSL:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowInputs ShowPreview InputsMode="BitColorInputsMode.Hsl" @bind-Color="hslInputsColor" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @hslInputsColor</BitLabel>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Presets" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
        <div>
            <b>Presets</b> puts a palette of one-click swatches under the picker, which is what turns "pick any color"
            into "pick one of ours" without giving up the gradient behind it. Each is written in any notation Color
            accepts, and the one matching the current color is marked with a ring. A preset carrying its own alpha
            applies it too, so the same hue can be offered at several transparencies; one that does not keeps the
            transparency already dialled in - and <b>transparent</b> is a color like any other, which is how a palette
            offers "no color" at all. <b>PresetsPerRow</b> lays the swatches on a grid instead of letting them wrap,
            keeping a palette meant to be read in columns in the arrangement it was written in.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            The palette is a single tab stop walked with the arrow keys - <b>Home</b> and <b>End</b> jump to its ends,
            and the vertical arrows move a row at a time once PresetsPerRow says how long a row is - so a thirty-color
            palette does not put thirty tab stops between the picker and whatever follows it. Tabbing in lands on the
            swatch the picker is already on.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <div>Brand palette:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowPreview Presets="brandPresets" @bind-Color="presetColor" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @presetColor</BitLabel>
            <br />
            <div>The same hue at four transparencies, plus "no color":</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowPreview ShowAlphaSlider Presets="alphaPresets" Format="BitColorFormat.Rgba" @bind-Color="alphaPresetColor" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @alphaPresetColor</BitLabel>
            <br />
            <div>A shade ramp, five to a row:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowPreview Presets="rampPresets" PresetsPerRow="5" @bind-Color="rampPresetColor" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @rampPresetColor</BitLabel>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Eye dropper" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
        <div>
            <b>ShowEyeDropper</b> adds the button that opens the browser's own eyedropper and samples a color from
            anywhere on the screen, including outside the browser window. It is a Chromium-only feature, so the button
            is rendered only where the browser actually provides one and the picker never offers a control that cannot
            work. Dismissing the eyedropper with Escape leaves the color where it was.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <div>Click the eyedropper, then click anywhere on the screen:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowEyeDropper ShowPreview ShowInputs @bind-Color="eyeDropperColor" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @eyeDropperColor</BitLabel>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Contrast" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
        <div>
            <b>ShowContrast</b> answers the question a color is usually being picked to settle: can this be read? It
            measures the picked color against <b>ContrastColor</b> - the background it will be read on, white unless
            you say otherwise - and reports the WCAG 2 ratio with whether it clears the bar for normal text (4.5:1)
            and for large text (3:1). A semi-transparent color is composited onto the background first, since a ratio
            taken against a color the eye never sees would answer the wrong question. Each verdict carries a mark as
            well as a color, and the readout is the saturation area's <b>aria-describedby</b>, so it is announced with
            the control it is about.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <div>Text color against a page background:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowContrast ShowInputs ShowPreview
                            ContrastColor="@contrastBackground"
                            @bind-Color="contrastColor" />
            <BitLabel>@contrastColor on @contrastBackground</BitLabel>
            <br />
            <div>Pick the background it is read on:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowPreview ShowInputs @bind-Color="contrastBackground" />
            <br />
            <div>Against a dark surface, with transparency in play:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowContrast ShowAlphaSlider ShowPreview
                            ContrastColor="#1B1A19"
                            Format="BitColorFormat.Rgba"
                            @bind-Color="contrastOnDarkColor" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @contrastOnDarkColor</BitLabel>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Format" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
        <div>
            Left unset, the picker answers in the notation it was given: a hexadecimal binding stays hexadecimal, an
            <b>rgb()</b> one stays <b>rgb()</b>. <b>Format</b> pins it to one instead, which matters when the value is
            going somewhere with its own expectations - a stylesheet variable, an API, a database column. Every
            notation has an alpha-carrying twin (<b>HexAlpha</b>, <b>Rgba</b>, <b>Hsla</b>, <b>Hsva</b>, <b>Hwba</b>,
            <b>Oklaba</b>, <b>Oklcha</b>): the plain ones drop the transparency from the string while the twins fold
            it in, which is what a single bound value needs to survive a semi-transparent color. <b>Hwb</b> is the
            "hue plus how much white and black" model, and <b>Oklab</b> and <b>Oklch</b> are the perceptually uniform
            pair modern design tokens are written in - a Tailwind palette is a list of <b>oklch()</b>. Both are read
            as well as written. <b>Hsv</b> is the model the picker itself is built on, but unlike the others it is not
            a notation any browser understands.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <BitColorPicker ShowAlphaSlider ShowPreview Format="selectedFormat" @bind-Color="formatColor" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @formatColor</BitLabel>
            <br />
            <BitChoiceGroup Horizontal
                            Label="Format"
                            @bind-Value="selectedFormat"
                            TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<BitColorFormat>" TValue="BitColorFormat">
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Hex" Value="BitColorFormat.Hex" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="HexAlpha" Value="BitColorFormat.HexAlpha" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Rgb" Value="BitColorFormat.Rgb" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Rgba" Value="BitColorFormat.Rgba" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Hsl" Value="BitColorFormat.Hsl" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Hsla" Value="BitColorFormat.Hsla" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Hsv" Value="BitColorFormat.Hsv" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Hsva" Value="BitColorFormat.Hsva" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Hwb" Value="BitColorFormat.Hwb" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Hwba" Value="BitColorFormat.Hwba" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Oklab" Value="BitColorFormat.Oklab" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Oklaba" Value="BitColorFormat.Oklaba" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Oklch" Value="BitColorFormat.Oklch" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Oklcha" Value="BitColorFormat.Oklcha" />
            </BitChoiceGroup>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Binding" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
        <div>
            A one-way <b>Color</b> with no callback beside it leaves the page in charge: the buttons below drive the
            picker, and its own controls are inert rather than pretending to accept edits they have nowhere to report.
            <b>@@bind-Color</b> completes the circuit, which is why the text field and the picker under it stay in step
            whichever one is used. That field is also the quickest way to see how forgiving the parser is: three-,
            four-, six- and eight-digit hexadecimal, <b>rgb()</b> and <b>rgba()</b>, <b>hsl()</b> and <b>hsla()</b>,
            <b>hsv()</b>, <b>hwb()</b>, <b>oklab()</b> and <b>oklch()</b>, <b>color(srgb ...)</b>, the modern
            space-separated syntax with a slashed alpha, any CSS color keyword, and <b>transparent</b> are all
            understood, and anything it cannot read leaves the picker where it was rather than throwing.
            <b>@@bind-Alpha</b> is the separate binding for the transparency, which a color string carrying its own
            alpha overrides.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <div>One-way:</div>
            <BitColorPicker Color="oneWayColor" ShowPreview />
            <div class="buttons">
                @foreach (var (label, color) in oneWayOptions)
                {
                    <BitButton OnClick="() => oneWayColor = color">@label</BitButton>
                }
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>Two-way (try 'tomato', '#0f8', 'rgb(255 128 0 / 40%)', 'hwb(280 10% 20%)', 'oklch(0.7 0.18 200)'):</div>
            <BitColorPicker @bind-Color="twoWayColor" @bind-Alpha="twoWayAlpha" ShowAlphaSlider ShowPreview />
            <BitTextField Label="Enter a color" @bind-Value="twoWayColor" Style="width: 220px;" />
            <BitLabel>Alpha: @twoWayAlpha</BitLabel>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Events & API" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
        <div>
            <b>OnChange</b> fires on every step of a drag, which keeps a live preview under the pointer.
            <b>OnChangeEnd</b> fires once the gesture is over - the drag ends, the slider is released, a field is
            committed, a preset is picked - and is the one to hang expensive work off; the counters below show how
            much more often the first runs. Both hand over a <b>BitColorChangeEventArgs</b> carrying the color in
            every notation at once, so a handler needing the hex does not convert the rgb back.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            A <b>@@ref</b> reads the same color without going through the binding, in whatever notation is wanted:
            <b>Hex</b> and <b>HexAlpha</b>, <b>Rgb</b> and <b>Rgba</b>, and the <b>Hsl</b>, <b>Hsv</b>, <b>Hwb</b> and
            <b>Oklch</b> tuples for the channels as numbers. <b>ColorDescription</b> is the color said in words -
            "light vibrant blue" - which is what the picker announces to a screen reader and what a page usually wants
            when it has to name the color in text. <b>FocusAsync</b> is on the same reference: it lands on the
            saturation area, which is what a picker revealed rather than rendered - opened in a popover, switched to
            in a tab - is focused with, since <b>AutoFocus</b> only fires on the first render.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <BitColorPicker @ref="colorPickerRef" ShowAlphaSlider ShowPreview
                            OnChange="HandleOnChange"
                            OnChangeEnd="HandleOnChangeEnd" />
            <BitButton OnClick="() => colorPickerRef?.FocusAsync()">Focus the picker</BitButton>
            <BitLabel>OnChange: @changeCount times, last @changedColor</BitLabel>
            <BitLabel>OnChangeEnd: @changeEndCount times, last @changedHex / @changedRgba</BitLabel>
            <br />
            <BitLabel>ColorDescription: @colorPickerRef?.ColorDescription</BitLabel>
            <BitLabel>Hex / HexAlpha: @colorPickerRef?.Hex / @colorPickerRef?.HexAlpha</BitLabel>
            <BitLabel>Rgb / Rgba: @colorPickerRef?.Rgb / @colorPickerRef?.Rgba</BitLabel>
            <BitLabel>Hsl: @colorPickerRef?.Hsl</BitLabel>
            <BitLabel>Hsv: @colorPickerRef?.Hsv</BitLabel>
            <BitLabel>Hwb: @colorPickerRef?.Hwb</BitLabel>
            <BitLabel>Oklch: @colorPickerRef?.Oklch</BitLabel>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Accessibility & localization" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode" Id="example10">
        <div>
            Every control is a tab stop of its own, and the saturation-brightness area is the interesting one: a
            two-dimensional control, which no ARIA role covers, exposed as a slider over the saturation and named a
            <b>2D slider</b> so the arrangement is announced. Its value text names the color before spelling it out -
            "light vibrant green, Saturation 75%, Brightness 80%, #5CCC33" - because a channel triplet is three
            numbers nobody can picture. The panel names itself the same way, and so does every preset swatch, so a
            palette is a list of colors rather than of hexadecimal strings read out digit by digit.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <b>ArrowLeft</b> and <b>ArrowRight</b> move the saturation by one percent, <b>ArrowUp</b> and
            <b>ArrowDown</b> the brightness; <b>Shift</b>, or <b>PageUp</b> and <b>PageDown</b> which need no
            modifier, moves ten at a time, and <b>Home</b> and <b>End</b> jump to the ends of the saturation axis. The
            horizontal arrows follow the reading direction, so they swap in right-to-left. The hue and alpha tracks
            are native range inputs and answer the whole keyboard contract of a slider for free.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <b>AriaLabel</b> names the panel, <b>Label</b> names it through the text it renders, and a picker given
            neither describes the color it is on. <b>AutoFocus</b> puts the focus on the gradient as soon as the
            picker renders, which is what a picker opened as the answer to a question - in a popover, a dialog, a
            toolbar - usually wants. A <b>ReadOnly</b> picker keeps every control focusable and announces itself as
            read-only rather than unavailable, since the color is the thing it is there to show.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <div>Tab to the gradient, then use the arrow, Page, Home and End keys:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowAlphaSlider ShowInputs ShowPreview
                            AriaLabel="Choose the brand color"
                            @bind-Color="accessibilityColor" />
            <BitLabel>Color: @accessibilityColor</BitLabel>
            <br />
            <div>Read-only: still reachable, still announced, refuses every change.</div>
            <BitColorPicker ReadOnly ShowAlphaSlider ShowInputs ShowPreview
                            Presets="brandPresets"
                            Color="@accessibilityColor" />
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            Almost everything the picker says is text it writes rather than text you hand it, so
            <b>Texts</b> is what translates the component: the accessible names of the controls, the captions
            of the fields, and the sentences the color is announced with. Its <b>Format</b> properties are
            <b>string.Format</b> templates, so a translation reorders what they interpolate - or leaves a
            placeholder out, which is how to drop the English <b>ColorDescription</b> from an announcement.
            Unset properties keep their English default, and the object usually travels through
            <b>BitParams</b> rather than being written on every picker.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div dir="rtl">
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitColorPicker Dir="BitDir.Rtl" ShowAlphaSlider ShowInputs ShowPreview ShowInputsModeSwitch
                                Texts="persianTexts"
                                Label="رنگ برند"
                                Presets="brandPresets"
                                @bind-Color="localizedColor" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" Id="example11">
        <div>
            <b>BitParams</b> carries a <b>BitColorPickerParams</b> down to every picker under it, so a panel, a form,
            or a whole page sets the shared shape - which controls to show, which palette to offer, which notation to
            publish - once instead of on every picker. What it carries is a default and not an override: a picker that
            writes a parameter for itself keeps its own value, and only what it left unset is filled in, which is what
            lets one step out of the group without the group knowing. The color itself is not on it: <b>Color</b> and
            <b>Alpha</b> are the value of one picker rather than a setting shared by all of them.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <BitParams Parameters="@colorPickerParams">
                <div>Takes the palette, the inputs, the preview and the format from the cascade:</div>
                <BitColorPicker @bind-Color="cascadedColor" />
                <BitLabel>Color: @cascadedColor</BitLabel>
                <br />
                <div>The same, without repeating any of it:</div>
                <BitColorPicker @bind-Color="cascadedOtherColor" />
                <BitLabel>Color: @cascadedOtherColor</BitLabel>
                <br />
                <div>Its own Size, the cascaded rest:</div>
                <BitColorPicker Size="BitSize.Small" @bind-Color="cascadedSmallColor" />
            </BitParams>
            <br />
            <div>Outside the cascade, and back to the defaults:</div>
            <BitColorPicker @bind-Color="cascadedSmallColor" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="External Icons" RazorCode="@example12RazorCode" Id="example12">
        <div>
            <b>EyeDropperIconName</b> replaces the built-in glyph of the eyedropper button with another from the bit
            icon set, and <b>EyeDropperIcon</b> takes a <b>BitIconInfo</b> instead, which is how an icon from an
            external library is used - <b>BitIconInfo.Fa</b> and <b>BitIconInfo.Bi</b> build the class names for
            FontAwesome and Bootstrap Icons, <b>BitIconInfo.Css</b> takes whatever classes you hand it, and the
            BitIconInfo wins when both are set. <b>InputsModeSwitchIcon</b> and <b>InputsModeSwitchIconName</b> do the
            same for the switch button. Remember to reference the stylesheet of the icon library itself. The
            eyedropper buttons below only appear on browsers that provide one.
        </div>

        <br /><br />

        <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/7.0.1/css/all.min.css" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap-icons@1.11.3/font/bootstrap-icons.min.css" />

        <div class="example-box">
            <div>bit icon set:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowEyeDropper ShowInputs ShowInputsModeSwitch
                            EyeDropperIconName="@BitIconName.Color"
                            InputsModeSwitchIconName="@BitIconName.Switch" />
            <br />
            <div>FontAwesome:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowEyeDropper ShowInputs ShowInputsModeSwitch
                            EyeDropperIcon="@BitIconInfo.Fa("solid eye-dropper")"
                            InputsModeSwitchIcon="@BitIconInfo.Fa("solid right-left")" />
            <br />
            <div>Bootstrap:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowEyeDropper ShowInputs ShowInputsModeSwitch
                            EyeDropperIcon="@BitIconInfo.Bi("eyedropper")"
                            InputsModeSwitchIcon="@BitIconInfo.Bi("arrow-left-right")" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Size" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" Id="example13">
        <div>
            <b>Size</b> scales the whole panel at once - the gradient, the tracks, the thumbs, the preview, the
            swatches and the type - from the <b>Small</b> that fits inside a narrow popover to the <b>Large</b> that
            carries a color asked as the main question on a page. <b>Medium</b> is the default. A larger size also
            means a larger gradient, which is the difference between picking a shade and approximating one.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <div>Small:</div>
            <BitColorPicker Size="BitSize.Small" ShowAlphaSlider ShowInputs ShowPreview Presets="brandPresets" @bind-Color="smallColor" />
            <br />
            <div>Medium:</div>
            <BitColorPicker Size="BitSize.Medium" ShowAlphaSlider ShowInputs ShowPreview Presets="brandPresets" @bind-Color="mediumColor" />
            <br />
            <div>Large:</div>
            <BitColorPicker Size="BitSize.Large" ShowAlphaSlider ShowInputs ShowPreview Presets="brandPresets" @bind-Color="largeColor" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example14RazorCode" Id="example14">
        <div>
            <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> reach the root element, where the panel's overall size and framing live -
            note that its height is the sum of its rows, so a fixed height shrinks the gradient rather than the
            controls. <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> go a level deeper and address the parts by name - among them
            <b>Root</b>, <b>SaturationPicker</b>, <b>SaturationThumb</b>, <b>HueSlider</b>, <b>AlphaSlider</b>,
            <b>Preview</b>, <b>Inputs</b>, <b>FieldInput</b>, <b>InputsModeSwitch</b>, <b>Label</b>, <b>Contrast</b>,
            <b>Preset</b> and <b>SelectedPreset</b>.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            For the values a design system sets rather than one instance, the picker reads a set of public
            <b>--bit-ColorPicker-*</b> variables (listed under <a href="#css-variables-section">CSS variables</a>).
            They are never declared by the component, so they inherit: set one on <b>:root</b> to re-skin every picker
            in the app, on an ancestor to re-skin the ones inside it, or on the <b>Style</b> of one to re-skin that
            one alone. It is also the answer to sizes the <b>Size</b> presets do not cover - a wider panel, a taller
            gradient, bigger swatches - since a size class and a variable never fight: the variable wins.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <div>Component's Style & Class:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowAlphaSlider Style="width: 230px; height: 150px;" />
            <br />
            <BitColorPicker ShowAlphaSlider Class="custom-class" />
            <br /><br />
            <div>Styles & Classes:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowAlphaSlider ShowPreview ShowInputs
                            Styles="@(new() { SaturationPicker = "border-radius: 1rem;",
                                              SaturationThumb = "width: 1.5rem; height: 1.5rem;",
                                              Preview = "border-radius: 50%;" })" />
            <br />
            <BitColorPicker ShowAlphaSlider ShowInputs Presets="brandPresets"
                            Classes="@(new() { FieldInput = "custom-field",
                                               Preset = "custom-preset",
                                               SelectedPreset = "custom-preset-selected" })" />
            <br /><br />
            <div>CSS variables, on one instance:</div>
            <BitColorPicker ShowAlphaSlider ShowInputs ShowPreview Presets="brandPresets"
                            Style="--bit-ColorPicker-width: 18rem;
                                   --bit-ColorPicker-saturation-height: 8rem;
                                   --bit-ColorPicker-saturation-radius: 1rem;
                                   --bit-ColorPicker-track-height: 0.75rem;
                                   --bit-ColorPicker-thumb-size: 1.25rem;
                                   --bit-ColorPicker-swatch-size: 1.25rem;
                                   --bit-ColorPicker-swatch-radius: 50%;
                                   --bit-ColorPicker-border-color: #7E4AE2;
                                   --bit-ColorPicker-focus-color: #7E4AE2;" />
            <br />
            <div>The same variables on an ancestor, re-skinning both pickers under it:</div>
            <div class="themed-pickers">
                <BitColorPicker ShowInputs ShowPreview Presets="brandPresets" />
                <BitColorPicker ShowInputs ShowPreview Size="BitSize.Small" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example15RazorCode" Id="example15">
        <div>
            <b>Dir="BitDir.Rtl"</b> mirrors the whole panel: the saturation axis runs from the right edge outwards,
            the hue and alpha tracks reverse along with the native sliders on them - so the fully transparent end
            stays under the position the thumb calls zero - the thumbs are corrected to stay centred on their color,
            and the horizontal arrow keys swap so that ArrowLeft still means "more saturated". The text fields and the
            presets flow right-to-left with everything else.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div dir="rtl">
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitColorPicker Dir="BitDir.Rtl" ShowAlphaSlider ShowPreview ShowInputs Presets="brandPresets" @bind-Color="rtlColor" />
                <BitLabel>@rtlColor</BitLabel>
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
</DemoPage>
